<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="chp-accessibility">
  <title>Accessibility and <pretext/></title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This handout is for workshop participants who are considering <pretext/>
      because of its accessibility features, or who need to meet institutional
      or legal accessibility requirements. It explains <em>how</em> <pretext/>
      achieves accessibility, which design choices are responsible for those
      gains, and what you, as an author, need to do to take full advantage of
      them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The short version: <pretext/> is built on semantic markup, which means
      most accessibility features are automatic. A few require a small amount
      of author effort — mainly writing short descriptions for images. The rest
      takes care of itself.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 1: What Accessibility Means for Math      -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-why-hard">
    <title>Why Math Accessibility Is Unusually Hard</title>

    <p>
      Most web accessibility problems are solved by adding descriptive text to
      visual content. Math is harder: a screen reader that reads
      <q>x squared plus two x plus one equals zero</q> gives a sighted reader
      everything, but it is almost impossible for a blind student to hold that
      structure in working memory long enough to factor it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The practical challenge for instructors is that standard accessible PDFs
      and web pages handle prose well but fail at math, data tables, and
      interactive figures — exactly the content that dominates STEM textbooks.
      <pretext/> was designed from the ground up to address this gap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 2: The Core Design Choice — Semantic Markup-->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-semantic">
    <title>The Core Design Choice: Semantic Markup</title>

    <p>
      The single most important design decision in <pretext/> is that authors
      describe <em>what content is</em>, not <em>how it should look</em>.
      Instead of writing <q>bold, indented block with a number in the margin</q>,
      you write <c>&lt;definition&gt;</c>. Instead of <q>centered italic line
      followed by indented text</q>, you write
      <c>&lt;theorem&gt;...&lt;proof&gt;</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This separation of content from presentation is what makes every
      accessibility gain described below possible. Because the system knows
      <em>what</em> something is, it can render it appropriately for each
      output format — screen reader, Braille embosser, large-print PDF, or
      interactive HTML — without any additional work from you.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="a11y-semantic-examples">
      <title>What Semantic Markup Looks Like</title>
      <p>
        <dl>
          <li>
            <title><c>&lt;definition&gt;</c></title>
            <p>
              Rendered as a styled, numbered block in HTML and PDF.
              Read aloud as <q>Definition N</q> by screen readers.
              Included in the definitions index automatically.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>&lt;m&gt;...&lt;/m&gt;</c> and <c>&lt;me&gt;...&lt;/me&gt;</c></title>
            <p>
              Inline and display math. Rendered visually via MathJax in HTML.
              The underlying MathML is exposed to screen readers, enabling
              structural navigation of equations.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title><c>&lt;image&gt;...&lt;shortdescription&gt;</c></title>
            <p>
              Images carry their own alternative-text metadata, making them
              accessible wherever the image appears — HTML, PDF, and ePub.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 3: Math Accessibility via MathJax/MathML  -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-math">
    <title>Math Accessibility: MathJax and MathML</title>

    <p>
      <pretext/> stores all mathematics as <latex/> source in the
      <c>&lt;m&gt;</c>, <c>&lt;me&gt;</c>, <c>&lt;men&gt;</c>, and
      <c>&lt;md&gt;</c> tags. When building HTML output, it uses
      <url href="https://www.mathjax.org">MathJax</url> to render equations
      in the browser.
    </p>

    <p>
      MathJax does more than draw pretty symbols. It simultaneously generates
      <url href="https://www.w3.org/TR/MathML3/">MathML</url> — a structured,
      machine-readable representation of the math — and attaches it to the
      rendered output. Screen readers that support MathML (including JAWS with
      MathPlayer, NVDA with MathCAT, and VoiceOver on recent macOS and iOS
      systems) can then navigate equations structurally: moving from term to
      term, zooming in on sub-expressions, and reading the equation at
      whatever level of detail the reader needs.
    </p>

    <p>
      MathJax also provides a right-click context menu on every equation that
      lets any reader copy the source, change the rendering mode, or zoom the
      equation — useful for students with low vision who do not use a screen
      reader.
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="a11y-math-author-note">
      <title>What You Need to Do</title>
      <p>
        Nothing. Every equation you write in a <c>&lt;m&gt;</c> tag is
        automatically accessible in HTML output. The MathJax and MathML
        infrastructure is part of the standard <pretext/> HTML build.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 4: Alternative Output Formats             -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-formats">
    <title>Alternative Output Formats</title>

    <p>
      Because <pretext/> source is semantic, it can be converted to multiple
      output formats from the same source file. Each format serves a different
      population of students.
    </p>

    <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>Format</cell>
        <cell>Who benefits</cell>
        <cell>How to generate</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Accessible HTML</cell>
        <cell>Screen reader users, keyboard-only users, mobile users</cell>
        <cell><c>pretext build web</c></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Print-quality PDF</cell>
        <cell>Students who prefer or require print; students with slow internet</cell>
        <cell><c>pretext build pdf</c></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>ePub</cell>
        <cell>Students using e-readers or read-aloud software on tablets</cell>
        <cell><c>pretext build epub</c></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Braille (BANA/Nemeth)</cell>
        <cell>Blind students; students who prefer tactile reading for math</cell>
        <cell><c>pretext build braille</c></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
      The Braille output is particularly significant. Most math textbooks cannot
      be converted to Braille without a specialist transcriber, which creates
      long wait times and high costs for students who are blind. <pretext/>
      uses the <url href="https://liblouis.io">liblouis</url> library and
      Nemeth Code rules to generate Braille from the same source as the HTML and
      PDF. An instructor who writes a <pretext/> textbook automatically provides
      a format that disability services can send directly to a Braille embosser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 5: HTML Structural Accessibility          -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-html-structure">
    <title>HTML Structural Accessibility</title>

    <p>
      The HTML that <pretext/> generates is structured to meet the
      <url href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG21/">Web Content Accessibility
      Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1</url> at the AA level. Key features include:
    </p>

    <dl>
      <li>
        <title>Heading hierarchy</title>
        <p>
          Chapters, sections, subsections, and named blocks each map to an
          appropriate HTML heading level (<c>h1</c>–<c>h6</c>). Screen
          reader users can navigate by heading, jumping directly to the
          section they need without reading the whole page.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Skip-navigation link</title>
        <p>
          Every page has a hidden <q>Skip to main content</q> link at the top
          that becomes visible on keyboard focus, letting keyboard users bypass
          the navigation sidebar.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>ARIA landmarks</title>
        <p>
          The page structure uses ARIA landmark roles
          (<c>role="navigation"</c>, <c>role="main"</c>, <c>role="search"</c>,
          etc.) so screen readers can provide a structural overview of the
          page.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Keyboard navigation</title>
        <p>
          The table of contents, knowl popups, and interactive exercises are
          all reachable and operable with the keyboard alone. No mouse is
          required.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Color contrast</title>
        <p>
          The default themes ship with foreground/background color combinations
          that meet the WCAG AA 4.5:1 contrast ratio for body text. Theming
          options (including a high-contrast mode) are available in the
          publication file.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <title>Responsive layout</title>
        <p>
          The HTML output reflows gracefully on small screens. Students using
          screen magnification software or a mobile device do not lose
          information when the viewport narrows.
        </p>
      </li>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 6: What Authors Must Do                   -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-author-responsibilities">
    <title>What You, the Author, Must Do</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <pretext/> handles structural accessibility automatically. There are
        two areas where author effort matters:
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="a11y-images">
      <title>Images</title>

      <p>
        Every image that carries information (as opposed to purely decorative
        dividers) needs a <c>&lt;shortdescription&gt;</c>. This becomes the
        <c>alt</c> attribute on the HTML <c>&lt;img&gt;</c> tag.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<image source="graph.png" width="60%">
  <shortdescription>
    A graph of f(x) = x squared on the interval [-2, 2],
    showing a parabola opening upward with vertex at the origin.
  </shortdescription>
</image>]]></code>
      </program>

      <p>
        For images that carry significant information — a complex diagram,
        a data table presented as an image, a geometric construction — add a
        <c>&lt;description&gt;</c> element as well. This longer text appears
        in the HTML as a separate expandable region that sighted readers can
        ignore and screen reader users can access.
      </p>

      <program language="xml">
        <code><![CDATA[<image source="venn.png" width="60%">
  <shortdescription>Venn diagram of sets A, B, and A intersect B.</shortdescription>
  <description>
    <p>
      Two overlapping circles. The left circle is labeled A and contains
      the elements 1, 2, 3. The right circle is labeled B and contains
      3, 4, 5. The overlapping region contains 3, representing A intersect B.
    </p>
  </description>
</image>]]></code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Figures generated by <pretext/> from <latex/> source (via TikZ or
        PGFPlots) are automatically converted to SVG, which is natively
        accessible and scales without blurring on high-resolution displays.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="a11y-video-audio">
      <title>Video and Audio</title>

      <p>
        Embedded videos should include captions and, where possible, a
        transcript. <pretext/> supports YouTube and Vimeo embeds; captions
        must be provided on the hosting platform (YouTube's auto-captions are
        a starting point but should be corrected before assigning to students).
        When you embed a video with <c>&lt;video&gt;</c>, add a
        <c>&lt;description&gt;</c> child element to provide context for users
        who cannot play the video.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="a11y-interactive">
      <title>Interactive Elements</title>

      <p>
        GeoGebra, Desmos, and JSXGraph applets are not inherently screen-reader
        accessible because they are visual by design. When you use these in a
        document you intend to be fully accessible, add a surrounding
        <c>&lt;p&gt;</c> or <c>&lt;remark&gt;</c> that describes what the
        interactive shows and what a student is supposed to observe from it.
        This ensures that a student who cannot interact with the applet still
        gets the mathematical content.
      </p>

      <p>
        Auto-graded exercises (true/false, multiple-choice, fill-in-the-blank)
        built with <pretext/>'s native exercise types <em>are</em>
        keyboard-accessible and screen-reader friendly when the document is
        hosted on Runestone Academy.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 7: Alignment with Guidelines              -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-guidelines">
    <title>How <pretext/> Aligns with Accessibility Guidelines</title>

    <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell>Guideline / Standard</cell>
        <cell>How <pretext/> addresses it</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>WCAG 2.1 Level AA</cell>
        <cell>
          Generated HTML uses proper heading hierarchy, ARIA landmarks,
          keyboard focus management, sufficient color contrast, and
          skip-navigation links.
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Section 508 (US federal)</cell>
        <cell>
          HTML output, MathML equations, and structured alt text satisfy the
          electronic content requirements of Section 508 Subpart B.
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>EPUB Accessibility 1.1</cell>
        <cell>
          ePub output includes navigation landmarks, MathML, and metadata
          conforming to the EPUB Accessibility specification.
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Nemeth Braille Code</cell>
        <cell>
          Braille output uses Nemeth Code for math expressions, the standard
          required by most US disability services for STEM materials.
        </cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Universal Design for Learning (UDL)</cell>
        <cell>
          Multiple output formats (HTML, PDF, ePub, Braille) and customizable
          reading options (zoom, color theme, font size) support UDL's
          principle of providing multiple means of representation.
        </cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </section>

  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <!-- Section 8: Quick Reference for Accessible Authoring -->
  <!-- ================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="a11y-quick-ref">
    <title>Quick Reference: Accessible Authoring Checklist</title>

    <p>
      Use this list when reviewing a <pretext/> document before publication.
    </p>

    <p>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Every informative image has a <c>&lt;shortdescription&gt;</c></title>
          <p>Purely decorative images should carry an empty <c>&lt;shortdescription/&gt;</c>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Complex images have a <c>&lt;description&gt;</c> element</title>
          <p>Diagrams, graphs, and tables presented as images need a full textual description.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Interactive applets have a surrounding prose description</title>
          <p>Students who cannot run the applet can still learn from the text around it.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Videos have captions on the hosting platform</title>
          <p>YouTube and Vimeo both support caption uploads. Review auto-generated captions for accuracy.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Tables use the <c>header="yes"</c> attribute on header rows</title>
          <p>This generates proper <c>&lt;th&gt;</c> elements that screen readers use to identify column headers.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>All cross-references use <c>&lt;xref&gt;</c>, not bare URLs</title>
          <p>Semantic cross-references produce meaningful link text (<q>Definition 3.2</q>) instead of raw URLs.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>The document builds without errors</title>
          <p>A document with structural errors may render correctly for sighted readers but produce broken ARIA or heading structure.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </section>

</chapter>
